message.content:
Reasoning:
- The contract has no constructor. `XBornID()` is not a constructor because its name does not exactly match `XBORNID`; it is a public function callable by any account. It sets `owner = msg.sender` and `balances[owner] = totalDistributed` with no access control, so untrusted `msg.sender` reaches both sinks.
- `onlyOwner` checks only `msg.sender == owner`. Any account can call `XBornID()` to set `owner` to itself and then pass `onlyOwner`. This makes `withdraw` and `withdrawForeignTokens` reachable by untrusted callers. `withdraw` transfers the contract Ether balance to `owner`; `withdrawForeignTokens` transfers the contract’s foreign token balance to `owner`.
- `getTokens` is public and `onlyWhitelist` only rejects blacklisted addresses. Its state changes to `totalDistributed`, `totalRemaining`, `balances`, `value`, and `distributionFinished` are part of the public distribution path; the function does not gate owner-only privileges, so these are not reported as access-control vulnerabilities here.

--Vulnerability--:
{Function:XBornID,Taint Path:msg.sender -> owner,Description:Public wrong constructor sets owner to msg.sender with no access control; any caller can become owner.}
{Function:XBornID,Taint Path:msg.sender -> owner -> balances[owner],Description:Public wrong constructor sets balances[owner] = totalDistributed with no access control; any caller can assign the distributed token amount to themselves.}
